Side (ally/enemy) determination — architecture correction (2026-08-16), before any TV Royale data was collected

Discussion with Duncan surfaced a real flaw in the just-built plan above: baking side into the training class taxonomy (`giant_ally`/`giant_enemy`) assumes a per-crop visual team signal exists. Duncan's first challenge: Clash Royale doesn't skin-tint troops by side — an isolated crop of an ally Giant and an enemy Giant likely render identically, so a classifier (side.onnx today, or a retrained model using this taxonomy) has no stable signal to key off. My first proposed verification (diff a real ally crop against a real enemy crop) was itself flawed, per Duncan's correction — troops face whichever way they're currently traveling roughly 80% correlated with side, but that's a confound (facing changes moment to moment — combat turns, knockback, pathing) not a team-identity signal, so that comparison would have measured facing direction, not side, and reinforced the wrong conclusion.

Duncan then supplied the actual answer, real game knowledge I didn't have: troops display a color-coded level number (red for enemy, blue for ally) above their head, plus a color-coded spawn "clock" icon briefly at placement, plus a color-coded health bar — with an asymmetric visibility rule that neatly explains this session's whole bug pattern: enemy troops ALWAYS show their (red) level number; ally troops show NEITHER level nor health bar until damaged (health under 100% OR over 100% from overheal). A full-health, undamaged ally troop therefore has NO team-color signal in frame at all — exactly the kind of edge case (fresh spawns, pre-damage) where this session's misclassification bugs clustered.

Revised plan, agreed with Duncan: (1) keep reusing build-a-bot's units.onnx for class+bbox — that part has been reasonably solid, it's specifically side.onnx that's the problem; (2) build perception/side_reader.py (planned, not started) as a direct algorithmic replacement for side.onnx — same "read an exact known pixel signal directly" reasoning perception/elixir_counter.py already validates, not a learned classifier. Reads the color-coded level-number/health-bar region at an offset RELATIVE to each detected unit's own bounding box (the icon moves with the unit, unlike elixir_counter.py's fixed-screen-position bar — a real difference in calibration approach, not just a copy of that module). Enemy's always-shown level number should make enemy-side detection close to fully reliable on its own, per Duncan's own read of the mechanic. The one remaining gap (full-health undamaged ally, no visual signal at all) falls back to context: own-play certainty from game_knowledge/cycle_tracker.py (we know when we place a card) plus track persistence (side gets assigned once per physical unit and inherited by its track thereafter, not re-derived from scratch every frame the way side.onnx effectively forces today) — Duncan's own point: this case "shouldn't be that bad" since we already know where/when we're placing our own troops.

Decision on the TV Royale pipeline: PAUSED, not scrapped, per Duncan's explicit question and my recommendation. side_reader.py is much cheaper to build/verify (a live calibration session, similar scope to hand_region.py/elixir_counter.py) than the full TV Royale pipeline's remaining live-calibration + macro-navigation work, and it directly targets what was actually the dominant bug class in every live test this session (unstable ally/enemy classification — the phantom-detection and class-hallucination bugs found via the ground-truth tooling are real but lower-frequency and orthogonal to side.onnx entirely, so side_reader.py won't fix those). Decision gate: if side_reader.py closes the gap well enough on its own, the TV Royale/custom-detector-training track may not be needed at all, or only much later for the remaining orthogonal problems (phantom detections, class hallucinations, the 5-card gap). If it doesn't, the already-built and -tested TV Royale pipeline pieces (perception/frame_diff.py, data/auto_labeler.py, both with real test coverage; data/tv_royale_watcher.py's structure) are still there, already corrected to the class-only taxonomy, ready to resume rather than rebuilt from scratch.

