Built as designed above: `detect_side(screenshot, bbox)` searches a region scaled to the unit's own bbox (small margin above, larger margin below/sideways — see the module's own `SEARCH_ABOVE_FACTOR` history for why "above" shrank from an original generous guess) for a red or blue blob passing color, size, and shape filters, returns `"ally"`/`"enemy"`/`None`. Verified first against the calibration screenshots from the session above (all passed, including the "deep in enemy territory" Giant), then wired into `perception/unit_detector.py` fully replacing `side.onnx`/`SideDetector` (deleted, not just bypassed) and `perception/unit_tracker.py` (a track's side now PERSISTS across `"unknown"` frames instead of requiring an exact match every frame — added `_side_compatible()`, treats `"unknown"` as a wildcard; verified synthetically that a track survives `unknown→unknown→ally→unknown` while a genuinely different unit still gets its own ID). `perception/debug_live.py` got a `side_color()`/`unit_view_color()` split, a `'u'`-key toggle between "side" view (blue=ally/red=enemy/orange=unknown, per Duncan's explicit color request) and "confidence" view (units.onnx's own detection confidence, reusing the card-matching green/orange/red scheme — added because the two meanings of green/red were being confused watching live), and `compact_tag()` now prefixes an `A`/`E` letter (also Duncan's request, redundant-with-color text for clarity).

First live-match test surfaced real bugs, found via three different investigation methods layered together this session, not guessed:

1. **Log-only analysis** (`logs/debug_live_20260816_171250.jsonl`, 1160 frames): confirmed no track ever flips `ally`↔`enemy` internally (the persistence logic works) — the "flickering enemy Giant" Duncan reported was actually the SAME physical unit's `detect_side()` read genuinely alternating frame-to-frame (`ally,ally,enemy,enemy,ally,ally,ally,ally` on a walking Giant, near-identical bbox each frame), not a tracking bug. A static screenshot of a similar Giant read cleanly — the instability is motion-specific, which a manually-timed capture can't catch on purpose.
2. **Auto-capture** (`find_side_conflict()` in `debug_live.py`, added specifically to solve problem #1): every frame, checks for two same-name tracks with different KNOWN sides within 100px, saves the raw frame + a JSON sidecar to `temp_img/side_conflicts/` (throttled to 1 per 3s). A second live match caught 37 real examples automatically — no manual timing needed.
3. **Direct pixel measurement** on the captured conflicts, per Duncan's explicit process rule (now in `CLAUDE.md`: always show him the actual image and let him confirm what's in it BEFORE acting on an interpretation) — this caught two real, different false-positive sources neither of us could have guessed from the log alone:
   - A Giant standing next to its own ally king tower had the tower's OWN always-on blue-trimmed structure/targeting-reticle picked up as a false "ally" signal (config/red_zones.py's `TOWER_DETECTION_EXCLUSION_ZONES` wasn't being applied to `side_reader.py` at all — fixed via `_suppress_tower_zones()`, zeroing out those pixels before the color search runs).
   - The SAME Giant's own orange HAIR (BGR≈(60,168,252), G/R=0.665) squeaked past the original fixed `RED_MIN_RG_GAP=80` threshold (R-G=84.4, just over). Fixed by switching to a RATIO check (`G < 0.5·R`) instead of a fixed subtraction, since a subtraction doesn't scale across the badge's filled (bright) vs. unfilled (dim) shades the same way a ratio does.
   - A completely separate case (two ally Archers, one damaged/correct, one undamaged/should-be-`unknown`) turned out to be reading an ENEMY KNIGHT's own boot as a false "enemy" badge — measured its color directly: BGR≈(45,64,178), G/R=0.356, genuinely INSIDE real badges' measured range (0.14-0.45). This is a hard color-space overlap, not a threshold-tuning problem — no amount of ratio/gap tuning can separate a reddish-brown boot from a crimson badge when their actual pixel values overlap.

That last finding is what motivated Duncan's own proposed fix — a second, independent verification layer on top of color: **digit-template matching**, reusing `card_detector.py`/`elixir_counter.py`'s exact `cv2.matchTemplate` (`TM_CCOEFF_NORMED`) technique rather than raw pixel-diff (which Duncan correctly flagged as fragile to overlap/lighting on his own, before being told the existing technique already solves that). Added to `side_reader.py`: `LEVEL_TEMPLATE_DIR="assets/level_badge_templates"`, digit values as subfolders (mirroring `elixir_templates/`), templates keyed by DIGIT ONLY (not color — the white numeral's shape doesn't change between a red or blue badge). `_badge_crop()` isolates just the square badge portion from a found blob (badge width ≈ its own height, even when merged with a variable-width bar) so two examples of the same digit at different health% still look similar to the matcher. `_blob_candidates()` (renamed from a single-best `_best_blob()`) now returns every shape-passing candidate, largest-first, so `_verify_badge()`/`_first_verified()` can reject the largest one (e.g. a boot) and fall through to the next real candidate instead of giving up. Graceful degradation is explicit: an empty template set always passes the crosscheck (never blocks color-based detection before any digit examples exist), same spirit as card/elixir matching reporting UNKNOWN rather than crashing with zero templates.

Bootstrapped with exactly one real template (a "3", cropped from a confirmed-good real capture and visually verified before saving) — enough to prove the mechanism works end-to-end: re-tested all 37 saved `temp_img/side_conflicts/` snapshots after adding the crosscheck and all 37 now resolve cleanly (up from 24/37 with the color-only fixes alone), including the Knight-boot case (now correctly `None`/`None` instead of a false `ally`/`enemy` split). `perception/side_reader.py`'s own `python -m perception.side_reader` now also replays every saved conflict snapshot as a standing regression check, growing automatically as `debug_live.py` captures more over time.

