=== DIRECTION CHANGE (2026-08-20): custom-trained detector is now the main line ===

After a live test of the KataCR detector, Duncan's call: **"continuing to train this
open-weight YOLO model on TV Royale games is definitely the way to go in the future."**
This supersedes the incremental heuristic-patching approach that produced most of the
"Next up" list below it. Everything in this section is the new plan of record.

Why the direction changed — the evidence, not just a preference:
- Our units.onnx self-reports `date: 2024-08-31`, YOLOv10m @ 480x352, 97 classes, and has
  NO classes at all for spells, health bars, level badges, or the deploy clock.
- KataCR (github.com/wty-yy/KataCR, MIT, 459*) ships two YOLOv8l @ 896 detectors covering
  155 classes, INCLUDING native classes for things this project hand-built entire
  pixel-calibrated modules for: `tower-bar`/`king-tower-bar` (tower_reader.py),
  `bar`/`bar-level` (side_reader.py), `clock` (spawn_icon_reader.py), `selected` (the
  hand-selection signal we'd previously confirmed nothing could see), and 19 spells (the
  entire never-built Tier 1/2/3 spell module).
- Live-tested by Duncan: "working much much better outside of the units it struggles
  with… it was even detecting enemy fireballs well."
- Measured: KataCR on GPU fp16 is ~20ms/frame vs units.onnx at ~32ms on CPU — more
  accurate AND faster.

**The decisive difference is that these weights are trainable.** Project_Status previously
named this the exact blocker: "build-a-bot only ships the exported ONNX (inference-only) —
no original trainable PyTorch checkpoint, no dataset, no training pipeline." All three now
exist: KataCR's `.pt` checkpoints carry real nn.Module weights (fine-tunable; the absent
optimizer state only prevents RESUMING a run, not fine-tuning), and their
Clash-Royale-Detection-Dataset is MIT with ~4,654 pre-cut unit sprites across 154 classes
PLUS the generator that composites them onto arena backgrounds.

That last point corrects a real earlier misjudgement recorded in this doc: the TV Royale
pipeline was designed around "a bounding-box annotation workflow doesn't exist yet" being
the hard blocker. The domain-standard solution is synthetic composition — cut each sprite
out once, paste onto backgrounds at known positions, and the labels are free and perfect.
That is how comparable projects get good results in days rather than months.

**Correction to an earlier claim in this doc (2026-08-20):** the persistence-gate fix was
justified partly by citing a rejected `electro_giant` as a "real play" being wrongly
downgraded. Duncan corrected this — there was no electro giant in that match at all. It was
a HALLUCINATION, and the gate was right to reject it. Tracing it in
logs/debug_live_20260820_032538.jsonl turned out to be more valuable than the original
claim: it was track id=6, Duncan's OWN ally Giant (alive since t=9.58, age 186 by then),
misread for exactly 2 frames as "electro_giant / enemy" before reverting to "giant / ally".
Two consequences, both since fixed:
- 2 frames satisfied the flat CLASS_LOCK_HITS/SIDE_LOCK_HITS debounce, so a 186-frame-old
  identity was overwritten by a blip. Fixed with age-scaled thresholds
  (ESTABLISHED_TRACK_AGE=25 / ESTABLISHED_LOCK_HITS=6): confidence in an identity should
  grow with the evidence behind it. Still finite, not a permanent lock — permanent locks
  caused this project's earlier side bugs.
- The accompanying SIDE flip made an established ally look like a brand-new enemy unit to
  opponent_play_tracker, which is exactly how a phantom play event is manufactured. This is
  the general mechanism behind several historical "phantom play" reports, not a one-off.
Verified separately that the visibility-based persistence check still rejects this
hallucination, so that fix did not regress into accepting it.

Known remaining detector gaps, all expected to be fixed BY training rather than around it:
- Misses our own archers and sometimes mini-pekka. Confirmed genuine recall failure: still
  absent at conf 0.15, not an NMS artifact, and unchanged by fixing a real preprocessing
  distortion (our arena crop stretches units 14.3% vertically vs 1.3% for their own
